title: Why a stream the processor decodes warns as slow
tags: [[viewer panel]]
kind: rationale
audience: dev
aliases: slow-decoder av1 dav1d why-only-measured-codecs-warn-as-slow
summary: A large video the processor decodes plays at a fraction of its frame rate in the web view, whatever the codec — so the probe warns above a pixel-rate budget unless a hardware decoder for the codec is installed.

A ''present'' decoder is not a ''usable'' one. `gst-discoverer-1.0` reports nothing
missing, the element is created, the file plays — at a few frames per second. And the
same file plays perfectly in mpv or smplayer, which decode on the graphics card and
draw there, where the web view decodes with whatever GStreamer element WebKit picks,
then converts every frame to RGBA and composites it in software. Nothing on screen
explained it.

So `GET /__media-probe` also answers `slow`: a message when the stream will be decoded
by the processor — no hardware decoder for its codec is installed — and its pixel rate
(width × height × frames per second) is over a budget of 150 Mpx/s. The [[viewer panel]]
shows it above the video and plays the file anyway — the user may only want to look
through it.

!! What was measured

Images actually rendered in the viewer panel (`getVideoPlaybackQuality`), on a 2-core
i7-7500U:

|!Stream |!Mpx/s |!Processor |!Graphics card (VA-API) |
|H.264 1920×1080 at 60 |124 |60 |60 |
|H.264 3840×2160 at 29.97 |248 |21 |30 |
|AV1 3840×2160 at 29.97, through dav1d |248 |about 20 |— (no AV1 in this GPU) |
|HEVC 3840×1920 at 59.94 |442 |10 |60 |

The codec hardly matters: what the processor runs out of is the pixels, and the
conversion and compositing cost the same for every codec. Hence one rule for all of
them, where there used to be a table of codecs measured one by one (AV1 only, on the
grounds that a threaded libav decoder keeps up — it decodes, but the web view does not
keep up behind it). dav1d no longer silences the warning for 4K AV1; only a hardware
decoder does.

!! The hardware decoders

`HARDWARE_DECODERS` (`crates/gui/src/media_support.rs`) lists, per codec, the elements
of VA-API (`gst-plugin-va`, and the older `gstreamer-vaapi`), NVDEC and V4L2. Each
plugin registers an element only for what the GPU actually decodes, so an element
present is a decoder that works. The warning suggests `gst-plugin-va` for a codec that
has some, and `gst-plugin-dav1d` besides for AV1 when no threaded software decoder is
installed — GStreamer's own, libaom's `av1dec`, is single-threaded.

!! What a wrong answer costs

* the budget is one machine's; a faster processor may play a 4K30 stream it warns about. It warns late rather than early on this one: 1080p60 stays silent;
* a decoder whose presence cannot be determined counts as ''present'' — no warning. For the output elements it is the reverse, since there a wrong ''present'' is a crash ([[Why a media file is not played on sight]]);
* a hardware element can be present and still refuse a profile (10-bit HEVC on an older GPU): then the file stutters with no warning;
* `slow` is never set when a decoder is missing: there is no playback to be slow.
